column1:
{
  "Text": "While both genders reported chest pain as the most frequent complaint, women more often added atypical symptoms. These vague complaints may have led clinicians to broaden the differential diagnosis and include non-cardiac diseases. Women reported a history of CAD less frequently than men. 
After accounting for these variables, women were still less likely to undergo diagnostic angiography than men.",
  "Assigned Label": "Bias",
  "Category": "Symptom Misinterpretation"
}

{
  "Text": "The primary influence of gender was the decision to perform coronary angiography. Although gender did not influence the decision to proceed with coronary revascularization, it did impact on the modality of coronary revascularization.",
  "Assigned Label": "No Bias",
  "Category": "Factual / Neutral Observed Outcome"
}

{
  "Text": "In contrast, in patients undergoing an initial strategy of PTCA, women were significantly less likely than men to undergo a subsequent revascularization, with a relative risk of 0.74 (P50.011).",
   "Assigned Label": "No Bias",
  "Category": "Factual / Neutral Observed Outcome"
}

{
  "Text": "It should also be acknowledged that we were able to track only clinically-driven TVR that might have been less in women, not because of less restenosis but rather because of fewer referrals for repeat catheterisations.",
  "Assigned Label": "Bias",
  "Category": "Diagnostic Uncertainty / Bias"
}

{
  "Text": "Women are less likely to have a discussion around risk factors for coronary artery disease compared with men.",
  "Assigned Label": "Bias",
  "Category": "Diagnostic Uncertainty / Bias"
}

{
  "Text": "Angiography was more consistently rated as having ‘high’ utility for male versus female patients.",
  "Assigned Label": "Bias",
  "Category": "Diagnostic Uncertainty / Bias"
}

{
  "Text": "Therapies for female-predominant phenotypes including nonobstructive coronary artery disease and heart failure with preserved ejection fraction are less well researched, contributing to undertreated female patients.",
  "Assigned Label": "Bias",
  "Category": "Diagnostic Uncertainty / Bias"
}

{
  "Text": "Women often have their presenting symptoms misattributed to non-AMI etiologies.",
  "Assigned Label": "Bias",
  "Category": "Symptom Misinterpretation"
}

{
  "Text": "Women are under-represented in cardiovascular trials, even when taking the prevalence to participant ratio into account.",
  "Assigned Label": "Bias",
  "Category": "Sampling Bias"
}

{
  "Text": "There are fewer proven therapeutic options for conditions with a female predisposition such as HFpEF and MINOCA because of fewer trials conducted and more negative outcomes in trials.",
  "Assigned Label": "Bias",
  "Category": "Sampling Bias"
}

{
  "Text": "Women are less likely to receive timely reperfusion therapy for ST-elevation myocardial infarction, coronary angiography for non-ST-elevation myocardial infarction, dual antiplatelet therapy, and secondary prevention therapies.",
  "Assigned Label": "Bias",
  "Category": "Diagnostic Uncertainty / Bias"
}

{
  "Text": "Provider bias in interpreting a greater number of presenting symptoms, some of which have been labelled as ‘atypical,’ can lead to mislabelling presentations as noncardiovascular.",
  "Assigned Label": "Bias",
  "Category": "Symptom Misinterpretation"
}

{
  "Text": "Coronary microvascular dysfunction and MINOCA, which are more common in women, often require additional testing that is not always considered standard of care.",
  "Assigned Label": "Bias",
  "Category": "Diagnostic Uncertainty / Bias"
}

{
  "Text": "Women with an AMI but no obstructive CAD are less likely to undergo additional diagnostic testing needed to identify MINOCA.",
  "Assigned Label": "Bias",
  "Category": "Diagnostic Uncertainty / Bias"
}

{
  "Text": "Since the nonobstructive type is harder to diagnose, women may not be diagnosed and treated as quickly as men.",
  "Assigned Label": "Bias",
  "Category": "Diagnostic Uncertainty / Bias"
}

{
  "Text": "Even women who have a history of CAD were less likely to report discussions around risk factors compared with men.",
  "Assigned Label": "Bias",
  "Category": "Diagnostic Uncertainty / Bias"
}

{
  "Text": "Women are under-referred for invasive cardiac procedures even when indications are present.",
  "Assigned Label": "Bias",
  "Category": "Diagnostic Uncertainty / Bias"
}

{
  "Text": "Women are 21% less likely than men to be told they are at risk for heart disease, 15% less likely to be told they are at risk of CAD, and are also less likely to self-identify as being at risk. This difference exists even after accounting for sociodemographic characteristics, access to health care, medical history, and the presence of traditional risk factors.",
  "Assigned Label": "Bias",
  "Category": "Diagnostic Uncertainty / Bias"
}

{
  "Text": "Despite the greater associated risk of diabetes to AMI for women, they are less likely to receive guideline-recommended care from their physicians.",
  "Assigned Label": "Bias",
  "Category": "Diagnostic Uncertainty / Bias"
}

{
  "Text": "Silent myocardial infarctions, which tend to be more prevalent among women, may not be identifiable from hospital records.",
  "Assigned Label": "Bias",
  "Category": "Diagnostic Uncertainty / Bias"
}

{
  "Text": "Women with an AMI but no obstructive CAD require additional testing that is not always considered standard of care.",
  "Assigned Label": "Bias",
  "Category": "Diagnostic Uncertainty / Bias"
}

{
  "Text": "The electrocardiograph is a cornerstone investigation in the diagnosis of AMI, however, electrocardiograph changes are less common for women with MINOCA, which might further contribute to provider bias in attributing presenting symptoms to noncardiac causes.",
  "Assigned Label": "Bias",
  "Category": "Symptom Misinterpretation"
}

{
  "Text": "Women, however, are more likely to describe their chest pain differently (tightness as opposed to pressure), present with additional symptoms such as jaw pain or dyspnea, and are therefore less likely to have their symptoms recognized as cardiac by treating physicians resulting in provider bias.",
  "Assigned Label": "Bias",
  "Category": "Symptom Misinterpretation"
}

{
  "Text": "Therefore, symptoms that might be categorized as ‘noncardiac’ on the basis of this definition might not truly be unrelated to cardiac causes for women.",
  "Assigned Label": "Bias",
  "Category": "Symptom Misinterpretation"
}

{
  "Text": "A contemporary meta-analysis of 2,264,600 primary care patients at high risk or with established cardiovascular disease showed lower prescription rates of aspirin, statins, and angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors in women compared with men.",
  "Assigned Label": "Bias",
  "Category": "Diagnostic Uncertainty / Bias"
}

{
  "Text": "At the patient level, women attend cardiac rehabilitation postevent to a lesser extent and are less likely to be followed by a specialist, particularly with nonobstructive syndromes.",
  "Assigned Label": "Bias",
  "Category": "Diagnostic Uncertainty / Bias"
}

{
  "Text": "Furthermore, there are data to suggest that providers are less likely to refer patients if they are older and are women.",
  "Assigned Label": "Bias",
  "Category": "Diagnostic Uncertainty / Bias"
}

{
  "Text": "The limited number of observations for younger veterans and active duty personnel with coronary heart disease is especially pronounced among women.",
  "Assigned Label": "Bias",
  "Category": "Sampling Bias"
}

{
  "Text": "Despite less time being spent discussing risk factors, paradoxically we know that women with CAD tend to have more risk factors than their age-matched counterparts.",
  "Assigned Label": "Bias",
  "Category": "Diagnostic Uncertainty / Bias"
}

{
  "Text": "Women are more likely to have prehospital delay in presentation after symptom onset (by ∼30 min compared to men), as well as underdiagnosis of myocardial infarction and lower priority for emergency ambulance services, while less likely to be presented to a percutaneous coronary intervention-capable facility 
and receive reperfusion therapy.",
  "Assigned Label": "Bias",
  "Category": "Diagnostic Uncertainty / Bias"
}

{
  "Text": "The limited number of observations for younger age bands, especially among women, could introduce error into our age-adjusted estimates.",
  "Assigned Label": "Bias",
  "Category": "Sampling Bias"
}

{
  "Text": "Since the nonobstructive type is harder to diagnose, women may not be diagnosed and treated as quickly as men.",
  "Assigned Label": "Bias",
  "Category": "Diagnostic Uncertainty / Bias"
}

{
  "Text": "After an abnormal cardiac stress test, women are less likely to undergo any subsequent diagnostic testing, including secondary stress testing or angiography.",
  "Assigned Label": "Bias",
  "Category": "Diagnostic Uncertainty / Bias"
}

{
  "Text": "Respondents were less certain of their CAD estimates for the female patient.",
  "Assigned Label": "Bias",
  "Category": "Diagnostic Uncertainty / Bias"
}

{
  "Text": "Cardiologists who associated risk taking more with men than with women were more likely to view angiography as highly useful for male versus female patients.",
  "Assigned Label": "Bias",
  "Category": "Diagnostic Uncertainty / Bias"
}

{
  "Text": "An assumption that all women with symptoms of angina or an abnormal stress test do not have obstructive CAD could result in significant health disparities.",
  "Assigned Label": "Bias",
  "Category": "Symptom Misinterpretation"
}

{
  "Text": "Physicians estimated the likelihood of obstructive CAD similarly but felt less certain of these estimates for the female patient.",
  "Assigned Label": "Bias",
  "Category": "Diagnostic Uncertainty / Bias"
}

{
  "Text": "Implicit gender bias explained some, but not all, of the gender variability in simulated clinical decision-making for suspected CAD.",
  "Assigned Label": "Bias",
  "Category": "Diagnostic Uncertainty / Bias"
}

{
  "Text": "The inappropriate application of prior knowledge or assumptions about differences between populations can result in knowledge-mediated bias. An assumption that all women with symptoms of angina or an abnormal stress test do not have obstructive CAD or are more risk avoidant, resulting in lower angiography 
rates in women, could result in significant health disparities.",
  "Assigned Label": "Bias",
  "Category": "Symptom Misinterpretation"
}

{
  "Text": "Until recently, few longitudinal studies of CAD in the general population have included women subjects.",
  "Assigned Label": "Bias",
  "Category": "Sampling Bias"
}

{
  "Text": "None of these variables entered the models, showing that depression and flights of stairs per day were risk factors exclusively for women and fibrinogen was important only in men.",
  "Assigned Label": "No Bias",
  "Category": "Factual / Neutral Observed Outcome"
}

{
  "Text": "Female sex is conventionally considered a risk factor for coronary artery bypass grafting and has been included as a poor prognostic factor in multiple cardiac operative risk evaluation scores.",
  "Assigned Label": "No Bias",
  "Category": "Biological / Physiological Differences"
}

{
  "Text": "Despite these facts, studies have suggested that physicians are less likely to pursue an aggressive approach to CAD in women than in men.",
  "Assigned Label": "Bias",
  "Category": "Diagnostic Uncertainty / Bias"
}

{
  "Text": "Some studies have shown higher rates of mortality and complications in women compared with men after coronary revascularization; however, after multivariable adjustment, female sex was often deemed not an independent predictor of poor outcome.",
  "Assigned Label": "No Bias",
  "Category": "Factual / Neutral Observed Outcome"
}

{
  "Text": "In STICH, the inclusion and exclusion criteria added to a protocolized application of medical therapy may have made women more similar to their male counterparts, and they may have received better medical therapy compared with cohort studies without those stipulations. Others have reported a lower use of 
medical therapy in women with CAD with less cardiovascular disease risk stratification.",
  "Assigned Label": "Bias",
  "Category": "Diagnostic Uncertainty / Bias"
}

{
  "Text": "Women consistently present with a higher burden of symptoms and comorbidities as compared with men and experience worse outcomes.",
  "Assigned Label": "No Bias",
  "Category": "Factual / Neutral Observed Outcome"
}

{
  "Text": "Potential explanations for this difference include differential diagnosis of CHD by physicians or differential treatment whereby physicians do not treat CHD as aggressively in women.",
  "Assigned Label": "Bias",
  "Category": "Diagnostic Uncertainty / Bias"
}

{
  "Text": "Women more often had non-obstructive CAD (17% vs. 5.4%, P < .01) and were less likely to have either three-vessel disease (15% vs. 20%, P = .02) or a significant LMCA stenosis (6.8% vs. 11%, P < .01).",
  "Assigned Label": "No bias",
  "Category": "Factual / Neutral Observed Outcome"
}

{
  "Text": "Obstructive coronary artery disease is more common in men, while nonobstructive coronary artery disease is more common in women.",
  "Assigned Label": "No bias",
  "Category": "Biological / Physiological Differences"
}

{
  "Text": "Women are more likely to have heart disease in the tiny arteries of the heart, called coronary microvascular disease. This can make the disease harder to identify and cause delays in treatment.",
  "Assigned Label": "No bias",
  "Category": "Biological / Physiological Differences"
}

{
  "Text": "The size and structure of the heart is different for women and men. A woman’s heart and blood vessels are smaller, and the muscular walls of women’s hearts are thinner.",
  "Assigned Label": "No bias",
  "Category": "Biological / Physiological Differences"
}

{
  "Text": "There was a trend for percutaneous coronary revascularization among women, and conversely, a trend for surgical revascularization among men.",
  "Assigned Label": "No bias",
  "Category": "Factual / Neutral Observed Outcome"
}

{
  "Text": "ACE-I and ARBs were least likely to be prescribed (63%), but again indistinguishable between genders [OR = 0.92 (0.77-1.09)]. There was no difference in the prescription of any of these medications by gender at the time of discharge.",
  "Assigned Label": "No bias",
  "Category": "Factual / Neutral Observed Outcome"
}

{
  "Text": "Once significant CAD was elucidated, women and men were equally referred for revascularization procedures.",
  "Assigned Label": "No bias",
  "Category": "Factual / Neutral Observed Outcome"
}

{
  "Text": "Numerous studies have shown that women undergoing coronary revascularization procedures do so at a higher risk for an adverse outcome compared with men.",
  "Assigned Label": "No bias",
  "Category": "Factual / Neutral Observed Outcome"
}

{
  "Text": "Women were older (64.0 versus 60.5 years), with more congestive heart failure (14% versus 7%), hypertension (68% versus 42%), treated diabetes mellitus (31% versus 15%), and unstable angina (67% versus 61%) than men.",
  "Assigned Label": "No bias",
  "Category": "Factual / Neutral Observed Outcome"
}

{
  "Text": "In patients undergoing an initial strategy of CABG, women were significantly more likely to undergo a subsequent procedure, with a relative risk of 1.74 (P50.043).",
  "Assigned Label": "No bias",
  "Category": "Factual / Neutral Observed Outcome"
}

{
  "Text": "In women undergoing PTCA, there was a higher incidence of congestive heart failure or pulmonary edema (4.8% versus 1.4%) than in men (P50.005).",
  "Assigned Label": "No bias",
  "Category": "Factual / Neutral Observed Outcome"
}

{
  "Text": "At 4 years, in patients undergoing an initial strategy of CABG, 18.8% of women compared with 13.2% of men (P50.047) reported angina, whereas in patients undergoing an initial strategy of PTCA, 23.3% of women compared with 19.7% of men (P50.27) reported anginal symptoms.",
  "Assigned Label": "No bias",
  "Category": "Factual / Neutral Observed Outcome"
}

{
  "Text": "The incidence of periprocedural congestive heart failure or pulmonary edema was significantly higher in both treatment strategies in women compared with men.",
  "Assigned Label": "No bias",
  "Category": "Factual / Neutral Observed Outcome"
}

{
  "Text": "Using an unselected PCI population, our gender-specific and stent-based risk-adjusted analysis indicates a profound prognostic advantage for DES compared to BMS in both genders. Female patients appeared to derive an even greater benefit from DES treatment.",
  "Assigned Label": "No bias",
  "Category": "Factual / Neutral Observed Outcome"
}

{
  "Text": "The benefits of reduced mortality and MI with DES were even more apparent among women, whereas the decline in TVR was more intense among men.",
  "Assigned Label": "No bias",
  "Category": "Factual / Neutral Observed Outcome"
}

{
  "Text": "Thus, our data encourage that the use of DES be maximised among both women and men in order to attenuate the potential difference in cardiac prognosis between genders.",
  "Assigned Label": "No bias",
  "Category": "Factual / Neutral Observed Outcome"
}

{
  "Text": "Sex influences pathogenesis, pathophysiology, presentation, postoperative complications, surgical outcomes, and survival.",
  "Assigned Label": "No bias",
  "Category": "Biological / Physiological Differences"
}

{
  "Text": "The short and long sleep duration increased the mortality of CAD in females, but the effect wasn’t found in males.",
  "Assigned Label": "No bias",
  "Category": "Factual / Neutral Observed Outcome"
}

{
  "Text": "After the subgroup analysis, the negative effect of short and long sleep duration was only observed for mortality of CAD in females.",
  "Assigned Label": "No bias",
  "Category": "Factual / Neutral Observed Outcome"
}

{
  "Text": "Men were treated more often for STEMI compared to women. However, the rate of PCI for acute coronary syndrome (including STEMI, non-STEMI, and unstable angina together) was similar for both women and men.",
  "Assigned Label": "No bias",
  "Category": "Factual / Neutral Observed Outcome"
}

{
  "Text": "Women were treated more often for proximal main vessel disease, but men were more likely to sustain three-vessel CAD.",
  "Assigned Label": "No bias",
  "Category": "Factual / Neutral Observed Outcome"
}

{
  "Text": "Earlier studies have suggested that women sustain increased mortality following PCI compared to men, which may be explained by differences in comorbid clinical conditions.",
  "Assigned Label": "No bias",
  "Category": "Factual / Neutral Observed Outcome"
}

{
  "Text": "CBF change was 2-fold larger in premenopausal women compared with age-matched men.",
  "Assigned Label": "No bias",
  "Category": "Biological / Physiological Differences"
}

{
  "Text": "Compared to men, women had less obstructive disease and a lower plaque mass.",
  "Assigned Label": "No bias",
  "Category": "Biological / Physiological Differences"
}

{
  "Text": "Intensive risk-factors’ modification had the same beneficial effects on plaque progression in both genders.",
  "Assigned Label": "No bias",
  "Category": "Factual / Neutral Observed Outcome"
}

{
  "Text": "Women with stable angina or acute coronary syndromes (ACS) experience higher in-hospital mortality, 1-year post-event mortality, and 5-year mortality after myocardial infarction (MI) as compared to men.",
  "Assigned Label": "No bias",
  "Category": "Factual / Neutral Observed Outcome"
}

{
  "Text": "The gender differences in clinical and angiographic outcomes following PCI seem to be more profound in acute coronary syndromes, particularly following MI events.",
  "Assigned Label": "No bias",
  "Category": "Factual / Neutral Observed Outcome"
}

{
  "Text": "Gender influences atherosclerotic coronary artery disease (CAD) risk, and may conceivably affect the efficacy of coronary revascularisation procedures.",
  "Assigned Label": "No bias",
  "Category": "Biological / Physiological Differences"
}

{
  "Text": "The impact of DES on clinical and angiographic outcomes in women is of particular interest because CAD is a major determinant of morbidity and mortality among both genders, but particularly among elderly women.",
  "Assigned Label": "No bias",
  "Category": "Factual / Neutral Observed Outcome"
}

{
  "Text": "Although angiographic success of attempted lesions was similar in women and men, angiographic success of intended lesions was higher in women because complete revascularization (number of intended lesions successfully dilated) was achieved more often in women.",
  "Assigned Label": "No bias",
  "Category": "Factual / Neutral Observed Outcome"
}

{
  "Text": "At an average of 5.4 years after revascularization, mortality and mortality plus myocardial infarction were the same for women and men in both treatment strategies.",
  "Assigned Label": "No bias",
  "Category": "Factual / Neutral Observed Outcome"
}

{
  "Text": "There was a similar occurrence of triple-vessel coronary disease and similar numbers of significant lesions, significant proximal lesions, significant proximal left anterior descending artery lesions, and total occlusions in women and men. Lesion morphology was also similar between groups.",
  "Assigned Label": "No bias",
  "Category": "Factual / Neutral Observed Outcome"
}

{
  "Text": "The mean number of significant lesions and the number of total grafts were the same in women and men.",
  "Assigned Label": "No bias",
  "Category": "Factual / Neutral Observed Outcome"
}

{
  "Text": "Although angiographic success in attempted lesions was similar between women and men (80% versus 78%), angiographic success in intended lesions was higher in women (75% versus 71%) than in men.",
  "Assigned Label": "No bias",
  "Category": "Factual / Neutral Observed Outcome"
}

{
  "Text": "Premenopausal women have sex-specific protective mechanisms against obstructive CAD of the epicardial vessels.",
  "Assigned Label": "No bias",
  "Category": "Biological / Physiological Differences"
}

{
  "Text": "Chest pain remains the typical symptom for both sexes who present with acute myocardial infarction.",
  "Assigned Label": "No bias",
  "Category": "Factual / Neutral Observed Outcome"
}

{
  "Text": "Angiographically, women are more likely to have nonobstructive coronary artery disease compared with men.",
  "Assigned Label": "No bias",
  "Category": "Biological / Physiological Differences"
}

{
  "Text": "MINOCA accounts for 10.5% and 3.4% of AMI presentations for women and men respectively.",
  "Assigned Label": "No bias",
  "Category": "Factual / Neutral Observed Outcome"
}

{
  "Text": "In young adults who present with acute myocardial infarction (AMI), paradoxically women are more likely than men to have diabetes.",
  "Assigned Label": "No bias",
  "Category": "Factual / Neutral Observed Outcome"
}

{
  "Text": "Women continue to have higher in-hospital, short-term, and long-term mortality rates than men after an AMI.",
  "Assigned Label": "No bias",
  "Category": "Factual / Neutral Observed Outcome"
}

{
  "Text": "Women are also at increased risk of serious side effects of reperfusion therapy including bleeding, particularly with thrombolytics.",
  "Assigned Label": "No bias",
  "Category": "Biological / Physiological Differences"
}

{
  "Text": "SCAD has a unique demographic and risk factor profile, with women comprising approximately 90% of cases.",
  "Assigned Label": "No bias",
  "Category": "Factual / Neutral Observed Outcome"
}

{
  "Text": "SCAD is believed to be the etiology of MI in 24–35% of women below the age of 60 years.",
  "Assigned Label": "No bias",
  "Category": "Factual / Neutral Observed Outcome"
}

{
  "Text": "SCAD is the most common etiology of MI in pregnant patients.",
  "Assigned Label": "No bias",
  "Category": "Biological / Physiological Differences"
}

{
  "Text": "Pregnancy-associated SCAD accounts for approximately 5–10% of all SCAD cases.",
  "Assigned Label": "No bias",
  "Category": "Biological / Physiological Differences"
}

{
  "Text": "Women have more microvascular dysfunction compared to men, which could be the basis of premature CAD.",
  "Assigned Label": "No bias",
  "Category": "Biological / Physiological Differences"
}

{
  "Text": "Women undergoing CABG tended to have more comorbidities at surgical presentation than men.",
  "Assigned Label": "No bias",
  "Category": "Factual / Neutral Observed Outcome"
}

{
  "Text": "However, studies focused on coronary syndrome in women corroborate the idea that women with comorbid depression have a higher incidence of cardiovascular events.",
  "Assigned Label": "No bias",
  "Category": "Factual / Neutral Observed Outcome"
}

{
  "Text": "Women are known to have smaller body surface area with relatively smaller coronary arteries; a more advanced and diffuse stage of coronary artery disease (CAD).",
  "Assigned Label": "No bias",
  "Category": "Biological / Physiological Differences"
}

{
  "Text": "According to the known risk stratification systems, such as the European System for Cardiac Operative Risk Evaluation and the Society of Thoracic Surgeons score, female sex is considered a risk factor for mortality after CABG.",
  "Assigned Label": "No bias",
  "Category": "Factual / Neutral Observed Outcome"
}

{
  "Text": "Women referred for coronary artery bypass grafting (CABG) have been reported to have higher perioperative mortality and morbidity compared to men.",
  "Assigned Label": "No bias",
  "Category": "Factual / Neutral Observed Outcome"
}

{
  "Text": "Women have smaller coronary arteries, which may make some diagnostic and therapeutic procedures less successful.",
  "Assigned Label": "No bias",
  "Category": "Biological / Physiological Differences"
}

{
  "Text": "Women tend to experience myocardial infarction 10 years later than men.",
  "Assigned Label": "No bias",
  "Category": "Biological / Physiological Differences"
}

{
  "Text": "Women have a greater incidence of diabetes when they experience myocardial infarction.",
  "Assigned Label": "No bias",
  "Category": "Factual / Neutral Observed Outcome"
}

{
  "Text": "Early large angiographic studies determined that women with typical angina had less obstructive CAD compared to men.",
  "Assigned Label": "No bias",
  "Category": "Factual / Neutral Observed Outcome"
}

{
  "Text": "Women are more likely to present with evidence of ischaemia and no obstructive coronary arteries compared to men, although they paradoxically have more chest pain.",
  "Assigned Label": "No bias",
  "Category": "Biological / Physiological Differences"
}

