{
  "Caption": "Table 1. A summary of outcome disparities categorized according to cardiovascular disease state and proposed interventions for addressing bias in each",
  "table": {
    "population": {
      "scope": "Cardiovascular disease entities; summary of sex/gender differences, sex/gender bias, and mitigation strategies"
    },
    "columns": [
      { "key": "cv_entity", "label": "Cardiovascular disease entity", "sublabel": "" },
      { "key": "sex_gender_differences", "label": "Sex and gender differences", "sublabel": "" },
      { "key": "sex_gender_bias", "label": "Sex and gender bias", "sublabel": "" },
      { "key": "mitigating_bias", "label": "Mitigating potential bias", "sublabel": "" }
    ],
    "row_groups": [
      { "key": "cad_ami", "label": "Coronary artery disease / acute myocardial infarction" },
      { "key": "nonobstructive_cad", "label": "Nonobstructive coronary artery disease" },
      { "key": "af", "label": "Atrial fibrillation" },
      { "key": "hf", "label": "Heart failure" },
      { "key": "pad", "label": "Peripheral arterial disease" }
    ],
    "rows": [
      {
        "group": "cad_ami",
        "label": "Obstructive CAD/AMI",
        "cells": {
          "cv_entity": "Obstructive CAD/AMI",
          "sex_gender_differences": [
            "The definition of “typical, atypical, and noncardiac” chest pain was derived from cohorts that predominantly included men, and is less predictive of obstructive CAD in women, especially those younger than 65 years of age",
            "Women continue to have higher in-hospital, short-term, and long-term mortality rates than men after an AMI"
          ],
          "sex_gender_bias": [
            "Women are less likely to have a discussion around risk factors",
            "Women are less likely to be considered at risk for cardiovascular disease by themselves and by their providers",
            "Women often have their presenting symptoms misattributed to non-AMI etiologies",
            "Women are less likely to attend cardiac rehabilitation, possibly influenced by social barriers (eg, taking time off, transportation challenges, payment)"
          ],
          "mitigating_bias": [
            "Consider public health campaigns to raise awareness of the prevalence of women’s heart disease",
            "Consider improved education and intensified intervention around sex-specific risk factors such as diabetes and psychosocial stress",
            "More research to inform sex-specific practice guidelines",
            "Consider improved education around female-specific obstetric risk and encouraging clinicians to obtain a complete obstetrical history (eg, gestational hypertension, eclampsia, gestational diabetes, preeclampsia, age of menopause) during index cardiovascular visits"
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "group": "nonobstructive_cad",
        "label": "Nonobstructive CAD",
        "cells": {
          "cv_entity": "Nonobstructive CAD",
          "sex_gender_differences": [
            "MINOCA accounts for 10.5% and 3.4% of AMI presentations for women and men respectively",
            "Up to 40% of the time, plaque rupture or erosion (female-prevalent conditions) is missed on 2-dimensional angiography; intracoronary imaging (eg, optical coherence tomography or intravascular ultrasound) is needed to diagnose this etiology"
          ],
          "sex_gender_bias": [
            "Identifying the underlying etiology of MINOCA often involves additional testing (eg, optical coherence tomography, coronary reactivity testing, positron emission tomography, MRI) that is not always considered standard of care in centers or providers of imaging"
          ],
          "mitigating_bias": [
            "In women with an AMI but no obstructive CAD, consider investigating for MINOCA early when no traditional CAD is found and/or referring women to a centre of excellence (eg, a women’s heart centre)",
            "Perform traditional and sex-specific risk factor screening",
            "Provide lifestyle education and medical treatment in women with MINOCA",
            "Increased use of and approval for diagnostic modalities optimized for the detection of nonobstructive CAD and MINOCA"
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "group": "af",
        "label": "AF",
        "cells": {
          "cv_entity": "AF",
          "sex_gender_differences": [
            "AF in women is associated with higher mortality than in men and greater stroke risk",
            "Anticoagulated and non-anticoagulated women remain at higher risk of stroke, particularly elderly women and those receiving warfarin as opposed to the newer oral anticoagulants",
            "Women with AF report more associated symptoms and describe their symptoms differently than do men"
          ],
          "sex_gender_bias": [
            "Women are less likely to seek investigations for symptoms and the time to diagnosis of AF is longer than for men",
            "Women are less likely to be prescribed oral anticoagulation even when comorbidities are accounted for",
            "Under-referral of women for ablation, despite higher symptom burden"
          ],
          "mitigating_bias": [
            "Consider more frequent and/or longer monitoring techniques in women with symptoms to detect AF earlier, particularly in those with less characteristic symptoms",
            "Consider prescribing newer oral anticoagulants in women because of excess mortality and bleeding risk in women with AF who are receiving warfarin",
            "Consider more research into why eligible women are under-referred for AF ablation"
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "group": "hf",
        "label": "HF",
        "cells": {
          "cv_entity": "HF",
          "sex_gender_differences": [
            "Women with HFrEF are less likely to receive target doses of guideline-directed medical therapy and device therapy",
            "Women are more likely to have HFpEF and are more likely to have a higher number of comorbidities"
          ],
          "sex_gender_bias": [
            "Cardiologists may have implicit bias viewing men as stronger and more likely to take risks, which may result in women being approached less often about device therapy",
            "No distinction of guideline therapies for HFpEF despite landmark trials showing sex differences regarding efficacy of therapies for HF populations that disproportionately affect female patients"
          ],
          "mitigating_bias": [
            "Consider closer monitoring of therapies to avoid adverse side effects and achieve optimal target dosing",
            "Shared decision-making around therapies and interventions even if perceived as invasive by provider",
            "More research into HFpEF therapies"
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "group": "pad",
        "label": "PAD",
        "cells": {
          "cv_entity": "PAD",
          "sex_gender_differences": [
            "PAD in women is diagnosed at a later stage with increased disease complexity, and is associated with increased perioperative complications"
          ],
          "sex_gender_bias": [
            "Lack of research into the factors contributing to sex and gender disparities",
            "No guideline directions on how to screen women for AAA, resulting in a bias toward under-recognition and undertreatment of women with arterial aneurysmal disease"
          ],
          "mitigating_bias": [
            "In women with risk factors for atherosclerotic vascular disease, consider screening for PAD with an ankle-brachial index, especially if age is 50 years or older",
            "Consider using body surface area–adjusted thresholds for intervention when risk-stratifying aneurysmal disease in women"
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
  "Assigned Label": "Bias",
  "Category": "Diagnostic Uncertainty / Bias"
}

