{
  "Caption": "Fig. 2. Challenges and disparities in treating coronary artery disease in women. ASA aspirin, CV cardiovascular, CAD coronary artery disease, HRT hormone replacement therapy, SCAD spontaneous coronary artery dissection, STEMI ST-segment elevation myocardial infarction.",
  "Figure": {
    "FigureType": "conceptual_flow_diagram",
    "CentralNode": {
      "ID": "central_theme",
      "Text": "Challenges and disparities in treating coronary artery disease in women"
    },
    "Nodes": [
      {
        "ID": "baseline_characteristics",
        "Text": "Women are older and have a higher burden of comorbidities at the time of CAD diagnosis compared to men"
      },
      {
        "ID": "ischemia_mechanisms",
        "Text": "Higher prevalence of atypical mechanisms of ischemia in women (e.g. microvascular dysfunction, coronary vasospasm, and SCAD) for which therapeutics are lacking"
      },
      {
        "ID": "rehabilitation",
        "Text": "Women may actually enjoy a greater benefit from cardiac rehabilitation than men but are less likely to be referred and less likely to attend or complete programs"
      },
      {
        "ID": "hormone_therapy",
        "Text": "Despite the potentially protective effects of estrogen in pre-menopausal women, HRT has not resulted in a reduction of CV events in menopausal women and is not currently recommended by guidelines for primary or secondary prevention"
      },
      {
        "ID": "diagnostic_access",
        "Text": "Women with suspected and known CAD are less likely than men to undergo coronary angiography"
      },
      {
        "ID": "stemi_care",
        "Text": "Women presenting with STEMI are less likely than men to be treated with a reperfusion strategy and experience longer door-to-balloon times"
      },
      {
        "ID": "guideline_therapy",
        "Text": "Women are less likely than men to receive guideline-directed medical therapy for CAD"
      },
      {
        "ID": "aspirin",
        "Text": "Aspirin: While the efficacy of secondary prevention ASA does not differ between sexes, the role of primary prevention ASA remains controversial, especially in women"
      },
      {
        "ID": "statins",
        "Text": "Statins: Some data suggest that statin therapy results in a greater degree of atheroma regression in women as compared to men. Clinical data and guidelines recommend equal treatment of men and women who warrant statin therapy. However, statins remain under-utilized in women as compared to men. Premenopausal women should be aware that statins are contraindicated during pregnancy"
      }
    ],
    "Connections": [
      {
        "From": "central_theme",
        "To": "baseline_characteristics",
        "Relationship": "contributes_to"
      },
      {
        "From": "central_theme",
