{
  "Text": "There is also underutilization in women of cardiac devices, including implantable cardiac defibrillators and cardiac resynchronization therapy (CRT.)",
  "Assigned Label": "Bias",
  "Category": "Diagnostic Uncertainty / Bias"
}

{
  "Text": "The previous assumption that heart disease in women is the same as that in men, only occurring about a decade later, represents an oversimplification and underscores the importance of sex-specific research in cardiovascular care.",
  "Assigned Label": "Bias",
  "Category": "Sampling Bias"
}

{
  "Text": "Females manifest non-obstructive CAD more frequently than males. They usually present atypical symptoms, including back pain, palpitations, etc., which lead to delayed diagnosis of the disease and consequently increase the risk of morbidity and mortality.",
  "Assigned Label": "Bias",
  "Category": "Diagnostic Uncertainty / Bias"
}

{
  "Text": "Clinical presentation is one of the factors that can influence how quickly a patient with an acute coronary syndrome is treated, particularly when this presentation is atypical.",
  "Assigned Label": "Bias",
  "Category": "Symptom Misinterpretation,"
}

{
  "Text": "The latter is more inclusive of disorders, such as microvascular and endothelial dysfunction, more commonly found in women, and the broader definition may improve the undertreatment and ultimately morbidity and mortality in women.",
  "Assigned Label": "Bias",
  "Category": "Diagnostic Uncertainty / Bias"
}

{
  "Text": "CR also imparts gendered influence on social support and psychological recovery after CABG, enhancing patient engagement and adherence with exercise and medication prescriptions. Although the benefits of CR are well established, most of these data (>85% of articles) come from studies in male patients.",
  "Assigned Label": "Bias",
  "Category": "Sampling Bias"
}

{
  "Text": "There are clearly gender-based differences documented, with fewer referrals to CR for women.",
  "Assigned Label": "Bias",
  "Category": "Diagnostic Uncertainty / Bias"
}

{
  "Text": "Roughly half of the patients were referred for CR, and only a third entered a CR program. Of those referred, 51 men (32%) and 9 women (23%) entered the 12-week CR program.",
  "Assigned Label": "Bias",
  "Category": "Diagnostic Uncertainty / Bias"
}

{
  "Text": "Conclusive evidence is limited from randomized controlled trials because the enrollment rate of women is <15%.",
  "Assigned Label": "Bias",
  "Category": "Sampling bias"
}

{
  "Text": "Gender differences have previously been described with CVD, with women 36% less likely to be enrolled in CR compared with men.",
  "Assigned Label": "Bias",
  "Category": "Diagnostic Uncertainty / Bias"
}

{
  "Text": "A 2007 study of Medicare patients found that 22% of men compared with 14% of women received referral for CR.",
  "Assigned Label": "Bias",
  "Category": "Diagnostic Uncertainty / Bias"
}

{
  "Text": "A decade later, another look at Medicare beneficiaries undergoing CABG found an increase in CR enrollment rates and yet persistent gender differences, with lower enrollment and retention in women compared with men.",
  "Assigned Label": "Bias",
  "Category": "Diagnostic Uncertainty / Bias"
}

{
  "Text": "We noted some variation in benefits of CR in men and women. For example, men experienced increases in METs (38%, P = 0.014), grip strength (11%, P < 0.0001), and sense of physical well-being (40.9%, P < 0.0001) (Table V). Women experienced predominantly increases in aerobic exercise duration 
(15.5%, P = 0.02) and a trend for improvement in sense for physical well-being (93.3%, P = 0.06) (Table V). It is possible that the inability to find gains in more domains for women is also a function of the small numbers of women completing the program in CR.",
  "Assigned Label": "Bias",
  "Category": "Sampling bias"
}

{
  "Text": "The inability to find gains in more domains for women is also a function of the small numbers of women completing the program in CR.",
  "Assigned Label": "Bias",
  "Category": "Sampling bias"
}

{
  "Text": "Importantly, in both trials women were a minority (CANTOS: 25.7% and COLCOT: ∼19%). To date, the data have not been split by sex.",
  "Assigned Label": "Bias",
  "Category": "Sampling bias"
}

{
  "Text": "Clinical studies investigating specific therapeutic interventions that target these steps in the inflammatory cascade have not been performed in single-sex groups.",
  "Assigned Label": "Bias",
  "Category": "Sampling Bias"
}

{
  "Text": "The hormonal status in the women was unknown, but no significant differences in clot lysability between the two age cohorts in women at both baseline and follow-up were observed.",
  "Assigned Label": "No Bias",
  "Category": "Factual / Neutral Observed Outcome"
}

{
  "Text": "Implicit gender bias does not explain all gender differences in CAD testing.",
  "Assigned Label": "No bias",
  "Category": "Factual / Neutral Observed Outcome"
}

{
  "Text": "The most notable finding was that the magnitude of the association of the locus on 10q11.21 with CAD was greater (P=0.015 for interaction) in women (OR=1.29 [1.15 to 1.45], P=1.86×10−5) compared with men (OR=1.03 [0.96 to 1.11], P=0.387).",
  "Assigned Label": "No bias",
  "Category": "Biological / Physiological Differences"
}

{
  "Text": "Women are less prone to CAD than men, which could partly be attributable to differences in gene–environment interactions. Interestingly, the locus on chromosome 10q11.21 showed a stronger association in women than in men. The nature of the locus with CXCL12 as the most proximate gene (Table 3) 
does not suggest an immediate mechanism that could explain a gender interaction and whether this finding, which was of borderline statistical significance and would not have been significant if we had adjusted for the multiple interaction analyses carried out, represents a true sex difference in effect requires further investigation.",
  "Assigned Label": "No bias",
  "Category": "Biological / Physiological Differences"
}

{
  "Text": "The 4 loci act independently with a combined OR of 1.15 (1.12 to 1.18) per additional risk allele. Because of the sex-specific effect of the locus on 10q11.21, the OR per additional risk allele was higher in women (1.21 [1.15 to 1.27]) compared with men (1.12 [1.08 to 1.16]). 
There was no significant interaction with age (P=0.30).",
  "Assigned Label": "No bias",
  "Category": "Biological / Physiological Differences"
}

{
  "Text": "However, at all ages, the prevalence of CAD in women is lower than in men, and the gender difference in plasma lipoprotein-lipid levels as well as in the prevalence of type II diabetes are believed to be responsible, at least in part, for the higher CAD risk observed in men.",
  "Assigned Label": "No bias",
  "Category": "Biological / Physiological Differences"
}

{
  "Text": "Indeed, men are characterized by an overall less favorable plasma lipid profile, which includes high fasting triglyceride (TG) and low HDL-cholesterol concentrations compared with women.",
  "Assigned Label": "No bias",
  "Category": "Biological / Physiological Differences"
}

{
  "Text": "An increased visceral adipose tissue (AT) accumulation has been reported in men compared with women and this factor could also contribute to the gender difference in the CAD risk profile.",
  "Assigned Label": "No bias",
  "Category": "Biological / Physiological Differences"
}

{
  "Text": "Indeed, men were characterized by an increased abdominal AT accumulation as expressed by higher waist circumference and waist-to-hip ratio values compared with women. Furthermore, men displayed a greater amount of visceral AT than women. In contrast, significantly 
higher levels of abdominal subcutaneous AT were noted in women compared with men.",
  "Assigned Label": "No bias",
  "Category": "Biological / Physiological Differences"
}

{
  "Text": "Gender differences in fasting plasma lipoprotein-lipid concentrations have already been reported.",
  "Assigned Label": "No bias",
  "Category": "Biological / Physiological Differences"
}

{
  "Text": "Men are known to present a preferential accumulation of AT in the abdominal visceral depot, whereas women are characterized by a more peripheral AT distribution.",
  "Assigned Label": "No bias",
  "Category": "Biological / Physiological Differences"
}

{
  "Text": "As women have less visceral AT than men, we tested the hypothesis that their preferential accumulation of subcutaneous AT would be associated with a more favorable postprandial TRL metabolism.",
  "Assigned Label": "No bias",
  "Category": "Biological / Physiological Differences"
}

{
  "Text": "The well known gender difference in visceral adipose tissue accumulation is an important contributing factor involved in the exaggerated postprandial triglyceride-rich lipoprotein response noted in men compared with women.",
  "Assigned Label": "No bias",
  "Category": "Biological / Physiological Differences"
}

{
  "Text": "Men show a greater postprandial triglyceridemic response to a meal than women.",
  "Assigned Label": "No bias",
  "Category": "Biological / Physiological Differences"
}

{
  "Text": "As women have less visceral AT than men, 39 we tested the hypothesis that their preferential accumulation of subcutaneous AT would be associated with a more favorable postprandial TRL metabolism.",
  "Assigned Label": "No bias",
  "Category": "Biological / Physiological Differences"
}

{
  "Text": "Alterations in plasma lipoprotein-lipid concentrations are known to increase the risk of coronary artery disease (CAD) in both men and women.",
  "Assigned Label": "No bias",
  "Category": "Biological / Physiological Differences"
}

{
  "Text": "Visceral adipose tissue was significantly associated with the postprandial triglyceride response in both genders.",
  "Assigned Label": "No bias",
  "Category": "Biological / Physiological Differences"
}

